WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.719
DNSSEC adds data-origin authentication,

2
00:00:02.719 --> 00:00:06.800
integrity protection and
authenticated denial of existence.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.522
It doesn't encrypt anything; it lets a
resolver verify the answer is authentic,

4
00:00:11.522 --> 00:00:15.875
and a validating resolver that finds
a bad signature answers SERVFAIL.

5
00:00:15.875 --> 00:00:21.508
NIST is explicit that DNSSEC and encrypted
DNS are complements: DNSSEC doesn't

6
00:00:21.508 --> 00:00:23.935
protect confidentiality, and DoH,

7
00:00:23.935 --> 00:00:27.662
DoT and DoQ protect the
transaction but not the data.

8
00:00:27.662 --> 00:00:29.026
The federal position.

9
00:00:29.026 --> 00:00:37.075
M-08-23 required DNSSEC by December
2009, with agency dot gov domains signed.

10
00:00:37.075 --> 00:00:42.524
SC-20 asks authoritative services
for origin and integrity artifacts,

11
00:00:42.524 --> 00:00:48.250
and SC-21 asks the recursive resolver
to request and perform verification.

12
00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:51.438
SP 800-81 Revision 3 recommends the root

13
00:00:51.438 --> 00:00:55.013
key as trust anchor with
automated rollover.

14
00:00:55.013 --> 00:00:58.389
On internal zones it's more
cautious than the whitepaper:

15
00:00:58.389 --> 00:01:03.273
signing them is likely of limited value
and recommended against unless required,

16
00:01:03.273 --> 00:01:07.727
because they may not chain to the root,
dynamic zones re-sign on every update,

17
00:01:07.727 --> 00:01:10.743
and signing is pointless
where authoritative and

18
00:01:10.743 --> 00:01:14.263
recursive service share servers,
as on domain controllers.

19
00:01:14.263 --> 00:01:17.825
Where required, use a delegation
that chains to the root.

20
00:01:17.825 --> 00:01:22.928
So the program signs external zones
always, validates on every agency resolver,

21
00:01:22.928 --> 00:01:27.800
and signs internal zones only where
the system's authorization requires it.

22
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:29.425
The platforms aren't equal.

23
00:01:29.425 --> 00:01:34.425
Windows Server uses a Key
Master, can replicate keys in AD,

24
00:01:34.425 --> 00:01:41.234
and supports RFC 5011 rollover; its
wizard's default 1,024-bit RSA zone-signing

25
00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:47.937
key is below NIST's floor, so the program
uses ECDSA or RSA of at least 2,048 bits.

26
00:01:47.937 --> 00:01:53.052
Azure DNS and Route 53 sign public
zones only; Azure's default

27
00:01:53.052 --> 00:01:58.575
resolver doesn't validate, and Route
53's ignores the DO and CD bits.

28
00:01:58.575 --> 00:02:01.680
Neither gives the agency
validation it controls.
